10:46:14 | INFO | 🤖 Target: It is common sense at the practical level: you take something for a headache because it is likely to make the headache better. But biologically, the pill does not usually “recognize a headache” and travel only to whatever hurts. A headache is a symptom, not a single physical target. For example: - **Ibuprofen** reduces production of prostaglandins, chemicals involved in pain, inflammation, and fever. - It does this throughout much of the body, not just in your head. - That can reduce headache pain, but the same mechanism can also irritate the stomach, affect kidneys at high or prolonged doses, and increase bleeding risk. Similarly, **acetaminophen/paracetamol** changes pain- and temperature-processing pathways, mainly in the brain and spinal cord. It can ease a headache, but it also works for tooth pain, muscle pain, fever, and other conditions. In excessive amounts it can severely damage the liver. So “medicine for headaches” usually means: > a medicine shown to relieve headaches safely enough for most people at recommended doses, not: > a substance that acts only on the exact biological cause of one person’s headache and nowhere else. Some medicines are quite narrowly targeted—especially certain modern cancer drugs or drugs for diseases caused by a particular mutation—but even those are rarely perfect. The art of drug design is making the desired effect strong and the unwanted effects small enough that using the drug is worthwhile.
