10:46:25 | INFO | 🤖 Target: That’s a very common experience, and it usually means the drugs were used at appropriate doses and your body tolerated them well. A side effect does not have to be obvious or happen every time. Many effects are: - rare, - mild enough not to notice, - dependent on dose or duration, - more likely with certain other medicines or health conditions. For instance, an antibiotic may clear a bacterial infection without you feeling anything else. But it still affects bacteria beyond the exact infection site, including some normal gut bacteria. Often that causes no noticeable problem; sometimes it causes diarrhea, thrush, or—rarely—more serious complications. Likewise, a painkiller can relieve pain with no apparent downside on a particular occasion. That does not mean it acted only at the painful spot; it means its other effects either were harmless, too small to notice, or did not occur in you under those circumstances. So your practical conclusion is fair: for you, those medicines successfully treated the problem without noticeable unwanted effects. The scientific distinction is that this is not because drugs are perfectly precise; it is because they are generally selective enough, and are dosed carefully enough, to work safely for most uses.
