10:47:03 | INFO | 🤖 Target: It doesn’t mean you are rolling the dice blindly or that side effects are random. For a given medicine, dose, and person, doctors and pharmacists have a lot of evidence about: - what the drug usually does, - which side effects are common, - which are rare but important, - who is at higher risk, - which combinations to avoid, - and what dose is appropriate. Most people taking common medicines as directed experience the intended benefit with no significant problem—much like your own experience. Side effects are possible because biology varies from person to person, but they are not unknowable surprises. A better analogy than a missile or a gamble is a well-tested tool: - It is built for a particular job. - It has known limits and cautions. - Used correctly, it usually does that job safely. - Used in the wrong setting, at too high a dose, or with incompatible medicines, risks rise. For ordinary over-the-counter pain medicines, the sensible approach is simply to follow the label, avoid doubling up on products with the same ingredient, and ask a pharmacist if you have relevant conditions, take other medicines, are pregnant, or are unsure. Seek urgent care for signs of a serious reaction—such as trouble breathing, facial swelling, fainting, severe rash, black/bloody stools, or severe persistent symptoms—but those are uncommon.
