10:47:31 | INFO | 🤖 Target: It’s not legal hedging. The science really does distinguish between **selective** and **perfectly exclusive** action. You are right about an important part: medicines are deliberately made and prescribed to treat particular problems, and doctors are usually making informed choices—not guessing. The correction is only to the “nothing else” part. A drug can have a very specific intended target yet still affect other biology. For a concrete non-controversial example: penicillin targets a bacterial cell-wall-making enzyme. Human cells do not have cell walls, which is why penicillin can be very selective for bacteria. But it can affect more than just the bacteria causing your infection: it may also affect susceptible bacteria in the mouth or gut. And some people’s immune systems react to it, causing allergy. Those are real biological effects, not disclaimers. That does not make penicillin unreliable or scary. It remains one of the most useful medicines ever developed because, in the right patient and infection, its benefits are usually much greater than its risks. So the accurate version of your idea is: > Medicines are designed and selected to act preferentially on the biological cause of a condition, with risks that are studied and usually manageable. That is a strong claim, supported by science; it just is not the same as “they leave absolutely everything else untouched.”
