10:47:45 | INFO | 🤖 Target: Yes—under that meaning, your point stands. Drug development starts with a specific intended job: identify a disease mechanism or symptom pathway, choose a target, and design or select a molecule to affect it. Developers then try to maximize the desired effect and minimize effects elsewhere. For example, an antibiotic may be designed to block a bacterial enzyme needed to build a cell wall. That is its intended target. Any effects on beneficial bacteria, allergies, or other unwanted outcomes are not the goal; they are limitations that testing tries to detect and reduce. The only nuance is that some medicines are designed around a broad effect rather than one exact molecular target—for example, many older painkillers or anesthetics. But they still have a defined therapeutic purpose. So in ordinary language: yes, medicines are made to address the problem they are prescribed for, not to cause unrelated effects.
