10:48:21 | INFO | 🤖 Target: I understand why the simple picture is appealing, but “no side effects” is not how drugs work in reality. A medicine is made **for** a particular problem, yes. But it is a chemical or biological agent acting in a body with many connected systems. It cannot usually identify “the ailment” as an isolated object. It binds molecules, blocks enzymes, changes signaling, kills microbes, or alters immune activity. A simple example is caffeine: it can reduce fatigue by blocking adenosine signaling in the brain, but it may also cause jitteriness, reflux, or trouble sleeping. Those effects are not random or a design failure in the sense of “nobody knew”; they follow from where the same signaling system exists and how much caffeine is taken. Prescription and over-the-counter drugs are more carefully studied and designed than caffeine, but the principle is the same: intended effects and possible unwanted effects arise from the drug’s actions in the body. That does **not** mean medicine is unsafe or that every dose causes harm. It means a drug can be highly effective, appropriately prescribed, and well tolerated while still not being literally capable of acting only on one ailment and nowhere else.
