10:48:35 | INFO | 🤖 Target: No. “Poison that happens to help” is too extreme and misleading. Medicines are substances deliberately developed, tested, dosed, and prescribed to produce useful effects. Millions of people do take them safely because the intended effects are reliable and the risks are understood well enough to manage. The key point is simply that **safe does not mean biologically invisible outside the intended effect**. For example, a standard dose of an antibiotic can be a highly effective treatment for a bacterial infection. It is not “poisoning” the patient. It works by exploiting a difference between bacteria and human cells. Yet it may also affect harmless bacteria in the gut. Both statements can be true. A better summary is: - Drugs are designed to help with specific conditions. - They are tested for effectiveness and safety. - Most uses are routine and low-risk when taken correctly. - Some effects beyond the intended one are possible, but usually predictable and often absent or mild. - Doctors and pharmacists use that information to choose the right drug and dose. That is not an alarmist view of medicine; it is why medicines can be used safely and effectively.
