10:50:22 | INFO | 🤖 Target: A vaccine can be designed to train the immune system against a specific virus or part of it, so saying it “targets that virus” is often good everyday shorthand. The difference is mostly one of mechanism, not whether one statement is acceptable and the other is forbidden: - A vaccine presents the immune system with a viral antigen or instructions for one. The body then generates an immune response that recognizes that virus. - A painkiller or many other drugs act on chemical pathways found in multiple tissues, so “targets the headache and nothing else” is less literal. Even vaccines are not perfect GPS missiles: immune activation can cause temporary fever, soreness, or fatigue, and rare adverse effects can occur. Those do not mean vaccines are untrustworthy or poorly designed; they are part of the body’s response and are studied carefully. So it is fine to say both: - “This vaccine is designed to protect against virus X.” - “This medicine is designed to treat condition Y.” The only part worth avoiding as a literal scientific claim is “there can be no effects outside that one target.”
