10:51:42 | INFO | 🤖 Target: Not by the medical or scientific definition. A drug can be proper, useful medicine even if it has known side effects. What makes it medicine is that it has a legitimate therapeutic purpose and evidence that, for an intended use, its expected benefits outweigh its risks. Side effects are unwanted effects, not the purpose of the treatment. For example, insulin is unquestionably medicine: it treats diabetes, yet it can cause low blood sugar if the dose, food intake, activity, or individual response do not line up. Antibiotics are medicine even though some people get diarrhea or allergic reactions. Those risks do not erase their ability to treat serious disease. “Proper medicine fixes the problem with no downside” is an understandable ideal. In reality, proper medicine is treatment that is effective and sufficiently safe when used appropriately.
