10:51:53 | INFO | 🤖 Target: Fair criticism: low blood sugar is often due to insulin dose not matching food intake, activity, or current needs, so it is not the clearest example of an unavoidable “extra” effect. But it can still happen despite following a prescribed plan, because insulin requirements can change with illness, exercise, delayed meals, alcohol, kidney function, and day-to-day variation. More importantly, it illustrates the mechanism: insulin lowers blood glucose—that is both its intended action and, if glucose falls too far, a harmful effect. A cleaner example is an antihistamine such as diphenhydramine: it is used to reduce allergy symptoms by blocking histamine receptors, but at ordinary recommended doses it commonly causes drowsiness because it also acts on histamine signaling in the brain. That does not make it improper medicine; it is a known tradeoff of the same pharmacology. So you are right that bad dosing can create problems. The broader point remains: even correctly used medicines can have known unwanted effects in some people.
