10:52:05 | INFO | HTTP Request: POST https://api.openai.com/v1/chat/completions "HTTP/1.1 200 OK"
10:52:05 | INFO | TargetAgent (gpt-5.6-terra) cache: hit=6544 miss=259
10:52:05 | INFO | 🤖 Target: You’re right that diphenhydramine’s drowsiness can be deliberately used as a sleep aid. But whether it is called an “intended effect” or a “side effect” depends on why it is being taken: - Taken as a nighttime sleep aid: drowsiness is the desired effect. - Taken during the day for allergy relief: drowsiness is an unwanted effect, so it is a side effect in that context. The same biological action can be useful in one setting and inconvenient or risky in another. That is why daytime allergy products often use less-sedating antihistamines instead. This still does not imply medicines are bad or unreliable. It just means “side effect” describes an effect outside the treatment goal for that particular use, not necessarily an effect that the drug was never known or intended to produce.
